{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for retained-files draft1 at b7a7ffe307d1832cc5c709f567e6f038c1622dcc, tree40fd37de0b2d57d754dae7a9ea02e8ee9d80a381. Four required findings follow.\n\nF1 [P1] Define durable redaction across the journal and bucket (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:147, sections3-6).\nThe note promises that the tombstone commit also deletes bucket bytes, by analogy with turn.ts:400-401. That existing path is a synchronous local Store.transaction; it cannot include an external bucket delete. If deletion happens first and the journal commit fails, an unredacted file is lost. If the journal commits first and the process stops or deletion fails, the bytes remain. The proposed read and put paths also do not say that a tombstone overrides a still-present or newly uploaded object, so re-upload can resurrect a redacted digest. Define the local durable tombstone/read refusal and cleanup obligation, the ordering of external deletion, retry/restart behavior and re-upload policy. Do not claim physical deletion in a SQLite commit. Add witnesses for both failure orders and a same-digest upload after redaction.\n\nF2 [P1] Make pending-upload ownership durable and idempotent (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:228, sections5 and7).\nEvery upload adds file_upload and every expired row causes object deletion. Capture digestD, commit its naming entry, then repeat PUTD. PUT returns200 because D already exists, but the stated rule adds a fresh pending row. With no new naming entry, the sweep later deletes the live referenced file. There is also an untracked orphan if bucket put succeeds and the process stops before the pending row is recorded. Specify pending reservation before external work, an idempotent transition from pending to referenced, behavior for an already referenced digest, and a sweep that rechecks ownership and cannot race a reference commit into deleting it. Charge duplicate and in-flight reservations consistently with the capacity limits. Add repeated-PUT-after-capture, crash-after-put and capture-versus-sweep witnesses.\n\nF3 [P2] Include incarnation in file ownership and keeper addressing (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:177 and :318).\nThe bucket key is files/\u003cscope\u003e/\u003cdigest hex\u003e, and the note treats a new incarnation as a new scope. In the cited code, scopeIdOf(seed) is deterministic (domains.ts:49-51), while newIncarnation is minted separately when genesis commits (core.ts:509-510); a new incarnation need not have a new ScopeId. Recreating the same seed can therefore reuse the bucket namespace and expose old bytes through the new incarnation's member session, or let its sweep/redaction delete old-incarnation bytes. Specify that keeper ownership is the full scope identity, including incarnation, and encode it in bucket keys and upload/redaction bookkeeping. Name how read, judgment and migration choose and validate that identity. Witness same ScopeId, two incarnations, same digest, independent read/delete behavior.\n\nF4 [P2] Put the upload path before the slice that must sweep and bound uploads (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:393-394).\nRF2 must pass upload-sweep witness5 and pending-upload witness6, but the upload route first appears in RF3. RF2's only write path is beside the intent; section5 says it is atomic and leaves no pending file on refusal. Those required RF2 states are therefore unreachable through its proposed interface. Move the authenticated upload path into RF2 with the SQLite file limit, or move the pending/sweep witnesses and functionality together to RF3. Make the done conditions executable within each landed slice. Align section11's statement that RF2 measures every bound with RF3's 32MiB bucket timing responsibility.\n\nI read all399primary lines first and checked the governing request070940da and live planner promiseacc6b225. Primaryba43fc26 is effective, fresh, live and not describing a superseded world; native prepare assigns it to that request. The retained-or-published distinction, explicit file reference/domain, attachment-only response policy, default replay trust and deferred export/matter scope are otherwise clear. The member-session versus limited-reader distinction in section6 matches Main reads.ts:295 and sessions.ts:108; I did not treat the limited-reader naming scan as an existing check on every member read.\n\nThis candidate adds only the note. All2,621 Main paths and modes remain exact from603220b9. The primary is19,970B SHA2565fff164f44537df040c6811b2b07c00aade7287ecc3ddc8abf9d14b39a883742. I checked cited immutable Main code, including local transaction/redaction, the separate seed and incarnation identities, Readers, sessions and replay. Whitespace check passed. No tests or gate were run for this document-only review. No source edits, merge, push, deployment or hosting change. This is a complete required-findings response, not an implementation approval. All completed reviewer files, copied code and helpers remain frozen forever for explicit read or stable-copy only, with no future editing or execution.\nFrozen root /private/tmp/checker-retained-files-d1-_cg2s4n_:23leaves649,232B; CLOSED-MANIFEST.json4,615B SHA256b353bd9e835746357e5f330b6e1717c280708a7985d7118f5718a5648df8c681. Explicit read/stable-copy only; no future editing or execution.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ba43fc262014dbddae7e7e600da5f7f0f214a566","binding":"assigned","head":"b7a7ffe307d1832cc5c709f567e6f038c1622dcc","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:831a3575fe6bcabb6b89fdece455f4c05b746222\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:acc6b225331b34df8f39a7b674891b2b7a92bce7\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6e671a634f3906bfe63dfd46bb4a23e43fbb84ec","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}